3 PAGE Typewritten History of the Duerksen Family in Alexanderthal, Molotschna Colony, Russia
3 DATA
4 DATE 29 December 1928
4 TEXT &quot;To Menno (Duerksen) from Uncle J. F. Duerksen - Esther (Esther Ruth Duerksen 1904-1998)&quot;
4 WWW http://person.ancestry.com/tree/52881559/person/13422621104/gallery
3 NOTE The source from which all Duerksen/Dirksen family histories prior to  1995 were drawn. In June 1995 California Mennonite Historical Society Bulletin #32 was published revealing many inaccuracies in the 1928 Duerksen Family History.
2 NOTE In his 1928 genealogy, John Funk Duerksen incorrectly called this Duerksen/Dirksen family progenitor Johann Duerksen. The 1835 Russian census of Molotschna identifies him as Kornelius.
1 BIRT
2 NOTE Tragheimerweide Church records recorded the surname as Diercks in 1801, then spelled it Dircks in 1809. Duerksen was a convenience for American typewriters. Sons of Heinrich Dircks (1809-1872) spelled it Dirksen on 1876 Dakota documents.
1 BAPM
2 DATE 1754 and 1756
2 PLAC montau, kr., Schwetz, Kujawsko-Pomorskie, Poland
2 NOTE Two entries for a Cornelius Dircks: 1  Mennonite Baptisms in Montau, West Prussia. 1741-1813, Goertz, Adalbert, LDS microfilm #72158. LDS microfilm #1573188, item 3
1 EVEN
2 TYPE Spelling the Surname
2 DATE Mid 1700s
2 PLAC Prussia
2 NOTE Prussian records spell this surname Diercks, or Dircks. GRanDMA OnLine) defaults to Duerksen. This man's descendants in Dakota Territory preferred Dirksen, while those in Kansas used Duerksen.
1 EVEN
2 TYPE GM# suffix
2 NOTE GRanDMA OnLine contains data for individuals in this Duerksen//Dirksen genealogy. The suffix GM# is added here to aid data confimation.
1 DEAT
2 DATE 09 Jul 1816
2 PLAC West Prussen, Deutschland
2 SOUR
3 _APID 1,61456::5342998
2 NOTE This 1816 death record is not yet confirmed to be of this man.
1 _DNA Direct link descendants of both of this man's sons have Y-DNA markers linked to Johan Kerber, a Mennonite born in West Prussia. In 1776 eight Kerber families lived near Grudzi♯�dz, part of the Montau Mennonite congregation.
