2 PAGE "Abraham Durksen was killed by lightning"  30 Sep 1886
2 DATA
3 WWW https://www.newspapers.com/article/turner-county-herald-abraham-durksen-wa/167031927/?xid=637
1 NOTE One of two Brotherfield Russian Mennonites killed by lightning in the 1880s. The other was Heinrich Vogt.
2 CONT    
2 CONT Birth Source: Brothers in Deed to Brothers in Need, page 295. Birth Source: Brother Jacob Dirksen&apos;s notes. Death Source: Sioux Falls Daily Press, 26 sep 1886. Abraham&apos;s age is incorrectly given as 40 (he was 33) on the Vaderland passenge
2 CONC r list in 1876. Abraham settled near his brothers and fellow immigrants. The Turner County line however, came to separate them so his original homestead is not in Brothersfield, but across the road, and county line, in Wellington Township of Minneh
2 CONC aha County. At the age of 43, Abraham was killed by lightning while on his way home from Hartford SD. His wife soon moved to Logan Co. ND where she homesteaded again with her 6 children. Nine years later she married Abraham&apos;s brother Jacob H
2 CONC . Dirksen after his wife Elizebeth Funk, Sarah&apos;s first cousin, died.
2 CONT    
2 CONT    
2 CONT About the immigration ship SS Vaderland:
2 CONT VADERLAND / GEOGRAPHIQUE 1872
2 CONT This was a 2,748 gross ton ship, length 320.5ft x beam 38.5ft, one funnel, three masts (rigged for sail), iron hull, single screw and a speed of 13 knots. Accommodation for 30-1st (later 70) and 800-3rd class passengers. Built by Palmers Shipbuildi
2 CONC ng &amp; Iron Co, Jarrow-on-Tyne, she was launched for the Red Star Line on 21st Aug.1872. Her maiden voyage started on 20th Jan.1873 when she left Antwerp for Falmouth, Halifax and Philadelphia. On 25th Mar.1873 she started her first Antwerp - Phi
2 CONC ladelphia direct sailing and on 29th Nov.1876 her first Antwerp - New York voyage. Between 1877-1887 she sailed Antwerp - New York or Philadelphia and commenced her last New York voyage on 26th Oct.1887. She subsequently sailed to Philadelphia unti
2 CONC l Mar.1889 when she was sold to French owmers and renamed GEOGRAPHIQUE. In Oct.1889 she was sunk in collision. [North Atlantic Seaway by N.R.P.Bonsor, vol.2,p.849/50]
2 CONT    
2 CONT A second twin stacked Vaderland was then built. Many sources incorrectly show a picture of it when referring to the immigrant ship SS Vaderland.
2 CONT The second steamship VADERLAND was built by John Brown &amp; Co Ltd, Glasgow, for the Red Star Line, and launched on 12 July 1900. 11,899 tons; 170,92 x 18,35 meters (560.8 x 60.2 feet, length x beam); 2 funnels, 4 masts; twin-screw propulsion, ser
2 CONC vice speed 15 knots; accommodation for 342 1st-, 194 2nd-, and 626 3rd-class passengers. 8 December 1900, maiden voyage, Antwerp- Southampton-New York (British flag). 11 December 1901, first voyage for the American Line (chartered), Southampton- Ch
2 CONC erbourg-New York. 8 April 1901, last voyage for the American Line, Southampton-Cherbourg- New York (3 roundtrip voyages). 16 May 1903, first voyage, Antwerp-New York (Belgian flag). 25 July 1914, last voyage, Antwerp-New York. 22 September 1914, fi
2 CONC rst voyage, Liverpool-New York (British flag). December 1914, first voyage for White Star-Dominion (chartered), Liverpool-Halifax-Portland. February 1915, last voyage for White Star-Dominion, Liverpool-Halifax-Portland (3 roundtrip voyages). 1915
2 CONC , renamed SOUTHLAND (International Navigation Co); taken up as a troopship. 2 September 1915, torpedoed in the Aegean Sea; reached port; repaired. August 1916, first voyage for White Star-Dominion (chartered), Liverpool-Quebec-Montreal. 4 June 1917
2 CONC , torpedoed and sunk by German submarine U.70 off the Irish coast (4 lives lost) [Noel Reginald Pixell Bonsor, North Atlantic Seaway; An Illustrated History of the Passenger Services Linking the Old World with the New (2nd ed.; Jersey, Channel Isla
2 CONC nds: Brookside Publications), vol. 2 (1978), p. 855]. Pictured in Arnold Kludas, Die grossen Passagierschiffe der Welt; Eine Dokumentation, Band I: 1858-1912 (2nd ed.; Oldenburg/ Hamburg: Gerhard Stalling, c1972), p. 83. [Posted to the Emigration-S
2 CONC hips Mailing List by Michael Palmer - 18 September 1997]
