1 NOTE &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/1953/alvin_gaede/&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;img border=1 width=200 align=right src=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/1953/alvin_gaede/cover.jpg&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt
2 CONC ;
2 CONT &amp;lt;br&amp;gt;Members of the &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/church_SDA/ShafterSDArecords1915-1923/&quot;&amp;gt;1915 - 1923 Shafter SDA German Church&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; and their descendants were par
2 CONC t of the &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/1953/shafter_reunion/index.html&quot;&amp;gt;1953 Shafter Duerksen Reunion&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;. They urged &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www
2 CONC .dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I17409&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;Dr. Alvin W. Gaede&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; to prepare a Duerksen family history. Gaede crafted the &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadi
2 CONC rksens.com/1953/alvin_gaede/&quot;&amp;gt;Johann Duerksen History (1750 - 1840)&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; from an &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/1953/marcellus_folder/marcellus_translation/all_1_page.htm&quot;&
2 CONC amp;gt;English translation&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; by &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I17855&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;Marcellus Duerksen&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; (and possibly &am
2 CONC p;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/1953/tabor_documents/I17819_middleton_adelaide_duerksen_her_translation.jpg&quot;&amp;gt;another translation&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; by &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&qu
2 CONC ot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I17819&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;Adelaide Duerksen Middleton&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;) of &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?perso
2 CONC nID=I16438&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;John Funk Duerksen&apos;s&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/1953/john_f_duerksen/&quot;&amp;gt;1928 Family History&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;.
2 CONT &amp;lt;p&amp;gt;These documents are preserved in the &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/1953/marcellus_folder/photos/index_img_26misc_contents.jpg&quot;&amp;gt;Marcellus Duerksen Folder&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; a
2 CONC rchived in the &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.fresno.edu/library&quot;&amp;gt;Hiebert Library&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;. They constitute the &quot;Duerksen Book&quot; (&amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dak
2 CONC otadirksens.com/1953/&quot;&amp;gt;View the Documents&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;). The later discovery of an &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/census/1835/&quot;&amp;gt;1835 Russian Census&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; of Molo
2 CONC tschna Colony revealed many conflicts and inaccuracies in Duerksen&apos;s 1928 papers.
2 CONT Alan Peters explained in the &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/census/1835/peters_alan_1835census_duerksens.jpg&quot;&amp;gt;June 1995 Mennonite Historical Society Bulletin&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;
2 CONT that the Duerksen progenitor is not the fictional Johann Duerksen (1750-1840) but in fact was &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I15459&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;Corneliu
2 CONC s Dircks&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; born about 1736.
2 CONT &amp;lt;p&amp;gt;Factual and  circumstantial evidence indicates that Cornelius Dircks had two sons, &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I14929&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;Jo
2 CONC hann Dircks&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; (1766-1855), later spelled Duerksen, and David Dircks (1761-1818). It was &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I16493&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;g
2 CONC t;David Dircks&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; who married three times, not the fictional Johann Duerksen. Children John Funk Duerksen had assigned to the fictional &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?person
2 CONC ID=I14929&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;Johann Duerksen&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;&apos;s fictitious 2nd and 3rd marriage were actually those of his brother &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?
2 CONC personID=I16493&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;David&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;.
2 CONT &amp;lt;p&amp;gt;In my view, &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I16438&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;John Funk Duerksen&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; mixed up the history of his great gr
2 CONC and parents &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I14929&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;Johann Cornelius and Katharina Dircks&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; in an understandable way. John Fun
2 CONC k would only have heard tales of his great grandfather &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I14929&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;Johann Cornelius Duerksen&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;, wh
2 CONC o helped establish &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/maps/Russia/MapAlex_1821.htm&quot;&amp;gt;Alexanderthal&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; in 1820. Johann Cornelius died eight years before John Funk was born. Perhap
2 CONC s inaccurate tales and recollections caused John Funk to write that his great grandfather had children from three marriages. In fact it was &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I16493&a
2 CONC mp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;David Duerksen&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; who married three times and was the father of many children listed as Johann&apos;s.
2 CONT &amp;lt;p&amp;gt;David died in Prussia, but his son &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I16498&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;Cornelius David Duerksen&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; had emi
2 CONC grated with Johann Cornelius to settle the new village of &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/maps/Russia/MapAlex_1821.htm&quot;&amp;gt;Alexanderthal&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;. Events described in &quot;&amp;lt;a ta
2 CONC rget=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/showmedia.php?mediaID=1340&amp;amp;medialinkID=4151&quot;&amp;gt;Mennonite Migrations&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;&quot; and the circumstance of their 1920 settlement of &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_
2 CONC blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/maps/Russia/MapAlex_1821.htm&quot;&amp;gt;Alexanderthal&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; provide a compelling argument that John Funk&apos;s great grandfather &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http
2 CONC ://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I14929&amp;amp;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;Johann Kornelius&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; was &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.dakotadirksens.com/getperson.php?personID=I16493&amp;amp
2 CONC ;tree=Brotherfield&quot;&amp;gt;David&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;&apos;s brother.
2 CONT &amp;lt;p&amp;gt;The &amp;lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;http://www.mennonitedna.com&quot;&amp;gt;Mennonite DNA Project&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt; may eventually prove or disprove that David and Johann were brothers. Until then I present them a
2 CONC s such in this genealogy.
2 CONT &amp;lt;p align=right&amp;gt;Mike Dirksen, 26 Aug 2011.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Only the children Johann, Heinrich and Eva survived to emigrate from Prussia to Russia with their parents in 1819. Eva died before 1835. Johann and Heinrich lived out their lives on Alexanderthal farm #11.
2 CONT * Birth Source:  http://www.dakotadirksens.com
2 CONT * Death Source:  http://www.dakotadirksens.com
2 CONT * Immigration Source:  Mennonite Migration to Russia, 1788-1828, page 117, 142, 155
2 CONT * Marriage Source:  Tragheimerweide, Prussia. Mennonite Church Records 1781-1862.
2 CONT * Notes:  Tragheimerweide records, page 198: surname is Dierksen. Pages 62, 67, 193 and 273: Diercks. Page 189: Dirck. 
2 CONT * Notes: The 1928 papers of John Funk Duerksen (http://www.dakotadirksens.com/photos/i16438_duerksen_marcellus_folder_images/i16438_duerksen_marcellus_folder_img_3.jpg) state, &quot;He is born - has lived and died at Marienwerder and Koenigsberg i
2 CONC n Prussia. His time 1765 to 1855. During the humbling of Germany by Napoleon, the French hordes came also through Prussia 1812 and the soldiers also went in (to his place) and made their quarters there. But being he was very liberal toward those pe
2 CONC ople, they have dealt with him in a friendly way. They paid for all that they used of his supplies. He was a handler of butter - cheese - and sausage. He had two sons.&quot; Duerksen&apos;s 1928 papers call him &quot;Johann J&quot; but the 1835 cen
2 CONC sus identifies him as Johann Kornelius. Duerksen&apos;s papers also say he was he was born about 1772, married 1 May 1795 and that he was the son of David Duerksen (#14628). However, that David was born about 1761, and cannot be his father. It is m
2 CONC ore likely David was a brother to Johann Kornelius, born about 1766. David died in 1818, then his sons Cornelius David (#14634) and Johann David (#60181), along with Johann Kornelius, emigrated in 1819 to Russia, with Elder Goertz&apos; Rudnerweid
2 CONC e group of 179 families. Johann brought his wife Katharina, sons Johann J. &quot;Oel-Ohm&quot;, Heinrich J. and daughter Eva, who died before 1835. Johann Kornelius, Cornellius David and Johann David were among the first sixteen families to settl
2 CONC e Alexanderthal (http://www.dakotadirksens.com/maps/Russia/MapAlex_1821.htm). The 1835 census shows Johann Kornelius at Alexanderthal #11, and Cornelius David Duerksen at farm #3, and Johann David at the #6 farm of his father-in-law Johann Ediger (
2 CONC #30337). Maria Duerksen Janzen #53991, whom John Funk Duerksen&apos;s 1928 papers call the sister of Johann Kornelius Duerksen, was in fact his wife&apos;s sister. Descendants of his elder son Johann emigrated to Kansas where they spelled the nam
2 CONC e Duerksen. Descendants of his son Heinrich emigrated to Dakota Territory and spelled their names Dirksen.
